  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14244490/assets/84c4df89c98cd103049ac6161c5d3e4eec258d3ecb3c1af94bf7d300f9342d22.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What are the values of $a,$ $b,$ and $k?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}},$\\,k=$\n{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=1.5,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=-1.$ This means that $k=1.5$ and $b=-1,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x-(-1)}+1.5=\\frac{a}{x+1}+1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(1,3.5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x+1}+1.5 \\\\ 3.5 & =\\frac{a}{1+1}+1.5 \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{a}{2} \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
